{"cells": [{"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "# Section 0 — IVP Foundations: Existence, Uniqueness, and Well-Posedness\n\n[← Module 3](README.md) ·\n[Next: Direction Fields →](01_direction_fields_autonomous_odes.ipynb)\n\nBefore approximating an ordinary differential equation numerically, we should ask\nwhether the initial-value problem has a solution, whether that solution is unique,\nand whether small perturbations lead only to small changes in the solution.\n\nConsider the first-order IVP\n\n$$\ny'=f(t,y), \\qquad a\\le t\\le b, \\qquad y(a)=\\alpha.\n$$\n\n### Learning goals\n\n- identify an initial-value problem (IVP);\n- test continuity of $f(t,y)$;\n- check a Lipschitz condition in $y$;\n- estimate a Lipschitz constant;\n- apply the existence-and-uniqueness theorem used in the course;\n- explain what it means for an IVP to be well-posed;\n- recognize how perturbations connect theory to numerical computation.\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 1. Lipschitz continuity in $y$\n\nA function $f(t,y)$ satisfies a **Lipschitz condition in $y$** on a set\n$D\\subset\\mathbb{R}^2$ if there is a constant $L>0$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t,y_1)-f(t,y_2)|\n\\le L|y_1-y_2|\n$$\n\nwhenever $(t,y_1)$ and $(t,y_2)$ lie in $D$.\n\nA practical test used in the course is:\n\n> If $D$ is convex and $\\partial f/\\partial y$ is continuous and bounded on\n> $D$, then $f$ is Lipschitz in $y$ on $D$.\n\nA valid Lipschitz constant is any number $L$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(t,y)\\right|\\le L\n$$\n\nthroughout the domain.\n"}, {"cell_type": "code", "execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": "import numpy as np\n\n\ndef max_abs_fy(f_y, t_values, y_values):\n    \"\"\"Estimate a bound for |partial f / partial y| on a grid.\"\"\"\n    t_grid, y_grid = np.meshgrid(t_values, y_values)\n    return np.max(np.abs(f_y(t_grid, y_grid)))\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 2. Review-style examples\n\nThe Exam 3 review asks whether several functions satisfy a Lipschitz condition on\n\n$$\nD=\\{(t,y):0\\le t\\le1,\ -\\infty<y<\\infty\\}.\n$$\n\n### Example A\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=t^2y+1.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=t^2.\n$$\n\nSince $0\\le t\\le1$,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right|\n=t^2\\le1.\n$$\n\nTherefore $f$ is Lipschitz in $y$ with $L=1$.\n\n### Example B\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=1-y.\n$$\n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-1,\n$$\n\nso $L=1$ works.\n\n### Example C\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=ty.\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=t,\n$$\n\nwe again have $|\\partial f/\\partial y|\\le1$ on $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nThese examples show an important point: the domain may be unbounded in $y$, but\nthe derivative test can still give a global Lipschitz constant if\n$\\partial f/\\partial y$ does not grow with $y$.\n"}, {"cell_type": "code", "execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": "t_values = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 101)\ny_values = np.linspace(-10.0, 10.0, 201)\n\nexamples = {\n    \"t^2 y + 1\": lambda t, y: t**2,\n    \"1 - y\": lambda t, y: -np.ones_like(t),\n    \"t y\": lambda t, y: t,\n}\n\nfor name, f_y in examples.items():\n    estimate = max_abs_fy(f_y, t_values, y_values)\n    print(f\"{name:12s}: estimated L = {estimate:.6f}\")\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 3. Existence and uniqueness\n\nThe theorem used in the course is applied on a rectangle or other suitable convex\ndomain containing the initial point.\n\nA useful version is:\n\n> If $f(t,y)$ is continuous on the domain and satisfies a Lipschitz condition\n> in $y$, then the IVP has a unique solution on the interval covered by the\n> theorem.\n\nThe two checks play different roles:\n\n- continuity of $f$ supports **existence**;\n- the Lipschitz condition in $y$ supports **uniqueness**.\n\nContinuity by itself does **not** guarantee uniqueness.\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 4. Where the conditions fail\n\n### No solution at the initial point\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{1}{t}, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(t,y)=1/t$ is not even defined at $t=0$, so the continuity\nhypothesis fails at the initial point.\n\n### Nonunique behavior\n\n$$\ny'=3y^{2/3}, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFormally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2y^{-1/3},\n$$\n\nwhich is unbounded near $y=0$. The practical Lipschitz test fails there.\n\nThe point is not that failure of the theorem automatically proves nonexistence or\nnonuniqueness. It means the theorem can no longer be used to guarantee the desired\nconclusion.\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 5. Well-posedness and perturbations\n\nAn IVP is **well-posed** when:\n\n1. a solution exists;\n2. the solution is unique;\n3. the solution depends continuously on the data.\n\nThe third condition matters numerically. A computer works with approximations, so\nsmall changes in the initial value or in the differential equation should not\nproduce arbitrarily large changes in the solution.\n\nA perturbed problem has the form\n\n$$\nz'=f(t,z)+\\delta(t),\n\\qquad\nz(a)=\\alpha+\\varepsilon_0.\n$$\n\nWell-posedness asks whether small $\\delta(t)$ and small $\\varepsilon_0$ produce a\nsolution $z(t)$ that remains close to the original solution $y(t)$.\n"}, {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": "## 6. A checklist for an exam problem\n\nFor an IVP\n\n$$\ny'=f(t,y), \\qquad y(t_0)=y_0,\n$$\n\nuse this sequence:\n\n1. **State the domain** $D$.\n2. **Check continuity** of $f$ on $D$.\n3. Compute $\\partial f/\\partial y$.\n4. Find a bound\n   $|\\partial f/\\partial y|\\le L$ on $D$.\n5. Conclude that $f$ is Lipschitz in $y$.\n6. Apply the existence-and-uniqueness theorem.\n7. If asked about well-posedness, explain continuous dependence on perturbations.\n\n### Check your understanding\n\nFor each function below on\n$D=\\{(t,y):0\\le t\\le1,\ -\\infty<y<\\infty\\}$, determine whether the derivative\ntest gives a global Lipschitz constant:\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=t^2y+1,\\qquad\nf(t,y)=1-y,\\qquad\nf(t,y)=ty,\\qquad\nf(t,y)=y^2.\n$$\n\nWhy is the last example different from the first three?\n\n### References\n\n- Leon Q. Brin, *Tea Time Numerical Analysis*.\n- Richard L. Burden and J. Douglas Faires, *Numerical Analysis*.\n- MAD 4401 lecture notes: IVPs, Lipschitz condition, existence/uniqueness,\n  and well-posedness.\n- MAD 4401 Exam 3 review.\n\n[Next: Direction Fields →](01_direction_fields_autonomous_odes.ipynb)\n"}], "metadata": {"kernelspec": {"display_name": "Python 3", "language": "python", "name": "python3"}, "language_info": {"name": "python"}}, "nbformat": 4, "nbformat_minor": 5}